      "id": 1,
      "text": "Van Nuys was founded in 1911 and named after rancher Isaac Newton Van Nuys, a Dutch‑ancestry entrepreneur.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Nuys",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1932 Art Deco Valley Municipal Building, now a Los Angeles Historic‑Cultural Monument, became the Valley’s first municipal civic center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Nuys",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "General Motors operated the Van Nuys Assembly plant from 1947 to 1992, producing cars on Van Nuys Boulevard and Arminta Street.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Nuys",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The former GM factory site now hosts The Plant shopping center, which opened in 1999 and includes a 16‑screen movie theater.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Nuys",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Van Nuys is the birthplace of Hollywood icons such as Natalie Wood, Robert Redford, Marilyn Monroe, and Jane Russell.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
